# Terminal contact pruning and the necessary nonlattice island

**Date:** 2026-10-11  
**Base:** `ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc`, state `2026-10-10.audit.234`  
**Target:** independent full-source Level I global route  
**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting promotion  
**Level I:** STILL OPEN. The nonlattice terminal-island case is not excluded.

## 1. The global chain

```text
triangle-free global minimizer
  -> finite full-source contact pruning with every actual diameter pair fixed
  -> terminal contact components rigid with their diameter endpoints pinned
  -> macroscopic component with linearly many actual diameter endpoints
  -> contradiction if its endpoint lattice has minimum spacing bounded below
```

The finite pruning is valid with any number of actual contact directions. No
ordinary rigidity of the entire source, common lattice, common phase, convex
contact complex, or absence of contact-isolated points is assumed. Every source
label remains present. This is a genuine global-minimizer implication, because
each constructed feasible endpoint has the same optimal diameter.

## 2. Dependencies and source check

- Canonical normalized KKT equilibrium: shared theory
  `01-local-structure.md`, Theorem 4.1.
- Elementary contact-component extent bound from the companion report
  `global_loaded_grid_contact_opening_2026-10-11.md`, Section 2:
  a positive-contact component incident to positive diameter stress has diameter
  at least gamma D, where gamma=sqrt(3)-1. This is a new unreviewed candidate
  dependency, not a canonical theorem. Its complete proof uses component force
  cancellation, Jung's planar radius bound, and variance Cauchy-Schwarz.
- Eppstein, *Edge Bounds and Degeneracy of Triangle-Free Penny Graphs and
  Squaregraphs*, JGAA 22(3), 483--499 (2018), **Theorem 2**, p. 491:
  a connected triangle-free penny graph on s vertices with graph diameter d
  has at most `2s-d-2` edges. The journal PDF was rechecked directly:
  <https://www.maths.tcd.ie/EMIS/journals/JGAA/accepted/2018/463.pdf>.
  This is also recorded in the canonical literature baseline, Section 2.4.
  The diameter d is the graph distance diameter, not the Euclidean source D.
  Radius-1/2 disks centered at unit-separated points give the same graph after
  rescaling the author's unit circles, so the graph statement applies unchanged.
- The lattice extreme-vertex argument below is elementary. The canonical
  `C-AFFINE-LATTICE-EXTREME-VERTEX-COUNT` already provides an analogous bound
  in inverse-basis-norm notation. No novelty priority is claimed for that count.

No unlisted report is used as a premise. The endpoint construction and rank
argument do not require any selected Target C representative.

## 3. Finite pruning with the exact diameter graph unchanged

Let A be a finite global minimizer of diameter D and let Q be all actual
diameter endpoints. At a current feasible placement define

\[
F_Q=\{w:r_{ij}\cdot(w_i-w_j)=0\ (ij\in C),\quad w_i=0\ (i\in Q)\}.
\]

Call an actual contact frozen when w_i=w_j for every w in F_Q. If some contact
is not frozen, choose one vector w in F_Q outside the proper subspace
`w_i=w_j` for every nonfrozen contact. Finitely many proper linear subspaces
cannot cover F_Q. A small straight motion gives

\[
|r_{ij}+t(w_i-w_j)|^2=1+t^2|w_i-w_j|^2
\]

on every old contact. Frozen edges stay exactly unit; every other contact opens.
All old noncontacts have positive lower margins, and all old pairs outside the
actual diameter graph have positive upper margins. Choose one positive t small
enough to preserve every full-source margin, including pairs between old contact
components and all contact-isolate pairs. Every actual diameter endpoint is fixed.
Thus the source cardinality, diameter D, exact actual diameter graph, and its
complete endpoint set Q are unchanged. The contact graph loses at least one edge
and gains none. Triangle-freeness is retained if it held initially.

Recompute F_Q at the new actual placement and repeat. This terminates after at
most the original number of contacts, because the contact sets strictly decrease.
Every endpoint is another global minimizer. Call the terminal placement A^dagger.
At that placement every remaining contact is frozen for the **terminal** F_Q.
Freezing at only the first step would not justify the terminal rank claim.

The procedure is also valid as a finite geometric operation on a nonglobal
feasible source; only preservation of global optimality and use of KKT at its
terminal endpoint require the original global-minimizer hypothesis.

## 4. Terminal components, rank, and original coordinates

Let J be a nontrivial terminal full-contact component and let P_J=J intersect Q,
s=|J|, q=|P_J|. An unpinned component could rotate about any center with all
contact length derivatives zero and nonzero contact-relative velocities; that
would contradict terminal freezing. Thus q>=1.

For any contact-bar flex of J fixing P_J, extend it by zero on the rest of the
source. This lies in the terminal F_Q. Every remaining contact has equal endpoint
velocities, so connectivity makes that velocity constant on J, and its pin makes
the constant zero. The reduced contact rigidity matrix therefore has rank

\[
\operatorname{rank}R_C[J\setminus P_J]=2(s-q).
\]

Let e be the number of contacts in J and e_PP the number with both endpoints in
P_J. The latter give zero rows in this reduced matrix, so

\[
e-e_{PP}\ge2(s-q).
\]

Every terminal component is a rigid translation of its original points at every
earlier pruning step: a final surviving edge survived all steps, so its endpoint
velocities were equal at each step. Its final paths propagate equality to an
original pin in Q, whose velocity was zero at all steps. Consequently **every
point of every nontrivial terminal component is at its original coordinate**.
Only points becoming contact isolates may have moved. This is stronger than a
claim that the source merely has an abstract isomorphic contact framework.

## 5. Universal linear loaded population in a macroscopic terminal component

Assume the original global minimizer is triangle-free. Normalize KKT at the
actual terminal endpoint. The companion extent lemma supplies a terminal
positive-contact component of diameter at least gamma D. Its containing full
contact component J also has that diameter and contains an actual diameter endpoint.
Write d for the graph diameter of J. Its edges have length one, hence
`d>=diam(J)>=gamma D`.

Apply Eppstein's connected penny-graph bound to this actual full-contact component:

\[
2(s-q)+e_{PP}\le e\le2s-d-2.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{2q\ge d+2+e_{PP}\ge\gamma D+2+e_{PP}.}
\]

**Universal theorem (proved candidate awaiting promotion).** Every finite
triangle-free global minimizer admits the above pruning endpoint with a
macroscopic terminal contact component J, at its original coordinates, containing
at least `gamma D/2+1` actual diameter endpoints. Its moving-column contact
matrix has full pinned rank, and every source point, including all old and new
contact isolates, remains in the configuration.

This conclusion does not bound the total nonisolated population, identify J with
a selected canonical C-II core, or produce a unit triangle. It applies to the
original minimizer without assuming that it already has a minimal contact count.

## 6. Minimum lattice spacing is the only lattice uniformity needed

Suppose P_J lies in a translate of a rank-two lattice whose shortest nonzero
vector has length rho>0. Every actual diameter endpoint is extreme in the full
source hull, so every point of P_J is a vertex of conv(P_J). If q>=64, set
`L=rho sqrt(q)/8`. At most

\[
4(L/\rho+1/2)^2\le9(L/\rho)^2=9q/64<q/2
\]

lattice vectors have length below L, by disjoint radius-rho/2 disk counting.
The oriented side vectors of the convex hull are distinct; at least q/2 sides
have length at least L. Perimeter at most pi D gives

\[
q\le(16\pi D/\rho)^{2/3}.
\]

For all q, including the collinear and small-cardinality cases,

\[
\boxed{q\le64+(16\pi D/\rho)^{2/3}.}
\]

Together with Section 5 this yields an explicit arithmetic obstruction. For
D>=512, gamma>1/2 gives q>D/4+1, hence
`(16pi D/rho)^(2/3)>D/8`. Since pi<4, cubing gives

\[
\boxed{D\rho^2<2^{21}.}
\]

Thus any containing lattice of this macroscopic terminal island must have
shortest-vector spacing O(D^(-1/2)). This is a necessary condition, not evidence
that such a containing lattice exists. A genuinely nondiscrete integer module
generated by contact directions need not fit any rank-two lattice.

## 7. Uniform exclusion of lattice-coherent contact components

**Theorem (proved candidate awaiting promotion).** Suppose every nontrivial
contact component of a finite triangle-free source lies in its own translate of
a rank-two lattice with shortest vector at least rho0>0. The lattices may have
different bases, orientations, phases, and arbitrarily many contact directions.
Contact-isolated points may lie anywhere. If

\[
D\ge\max\{512,2^{21}/\rho_0^2\},
\]

the source cannot be a global diameter minimizer.

**Proof.** If it were global, Section 5 would produce the terminal component J.
It is an unchanged subset of one original contact component, so its pin set lies
in that component's original lattice. Section 6 would require
`D rho^2<2^21`, contradicting rho>=rho0 and the displayed threshold.

In particular every sufficiently large global minimizer contained in any
uniformly separated rank-two lattice contains a unit equilateral triangle.
This includes triangular lattices, general lattice bases, nonconvex subsets,
holes, varying source phases, and all contact isolates. The spacing condition
must hold uniformly; putting rational coordinates in increasingly fine lattices
does not meet it. The theorem removes whole-source ordinary-rigidity assumptions
from this exact lattice scope. It does not assert global crystallization.

The canonical lattice attachment obstruction works with a selected post-ZF
representative and its actual harmonic attachments. The present proof is a
separate full-source normalization argument, preserving the original diameter
endpoints and proving the pinned-rank premise it uses. No canonical lifecycle
or route endpoint is changed by recording it.

## 8. Exact finite certificate and binding controls

`research/verify_global_terminal_contact_pruning_2026_10_11.py` implements exact
rational nullspaces, pointwise frozen-contact detection, a deterministic generic
velocity, and finite full-pair margin checks at every pruning round. Exact minima
of the pair-distance quadratics certify lower feasibility over each entire motion
interval; convexity certifies upper feasibility from the two endpoints. It retains
all labels and checks the unchanged full diameter graph, terminal pinned rank,
and the graph-diameter edge inequalities.

- The 2x3 square source needs two rounds: first its vertical contacts open,
  then its unpinned middle horizontal pair opens. Its two pinned horizontal
  pairs remain. A certified final vertical contraction gives a strict competitor.
- A square source with a central hole, a rational third-direction contact leaf,
  and an arbitrary off-lattice isolate tests contact directions beyond the
  preceding two-coordinate chart. Its proper old KKT stress is checked exactly;
  contact pruning opens the old contacts, and a rational contraction improves D.
- A rational unit hexagon with three directions has zero pinned bar kernel.
  Pruning does nothing, but its full equilibrium matrix has independent columns,
  so it has no nonzero equilibrium stress and no normalized KKT certificate.
  Six triangular-lattice points with squared diameter four give a strict
  independent competitor. This prevents mistaking frozen or pinned-rigid
  configurations for global minimizers.
- The optimal unit square is an unchanged control with its actual positive KKT
  stress. The method does not reject that finite triangle-free optimum.

These finite fixtures do not certify a large global minimizer, the general
asymptotic exclusion, or Level I. The general proof is analytical above.

## 9. Remaining global case

A hypothetical unbounded triangle-free global-minimizer family must now have
the macroscopic terminal island of Section 5, with linearly many original
diameter endpoints, and no containing lattice of uniformly positive minimum
spacing. This can be a genuinely nonlattice contact framework. The proof gives
no global same-cardinality replacement for that case.

The full goal remains to construct such a replacement of diameter below D or
prove that every sufficiently large global minimizer's terminal contact graph
contains a unit triangle. An unrestricted replacement may move every old
diameter endpoint. The pruning normalization is a necessary-condition tool,
not a restriction on the allowed final solution.

Promotion recommendation: preserve as an unreviewed proved candidate, obtain an
independent proof/dependency/scope audit, and do not claim Level I closure.
